window.EXTRA = window.EXTRA || {};
EXTRA.dm = {
  1: [
    { q: `A set S has 5 elements. How many elements does its power set P(S) have?`,
      o: [[`32`, `Correct. If |S| = n then |P(S)| = 2ⁿ, because each element is either in or out of a subset: 2⁵ = 32 (L1, Power set).`], [`31`, `31 = 2⁵ − 1 leaves out the empty set, but ∅ is always a member of P(S). The full count is 2⁵ = 32.`], [`10`, `10 = 2 × 5 multiplies instead of raising to a power. The rule is 2ⁿ, so 2⁵ = 32.`], [`25`, `25 = 5² swaps base and exponent. The rule is |P(S)| = 2ⁿ, so 2⁵ = 32.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `Which of the following sets is empty?`,
      o: [[`{x : x ∈ N, x &lt; 2}`, `With N = {1, 2, 3, …}, the number 1 satisfies x &lt; 2, so this set is {1}, which is not empty.`], [`{x : x ∈ N, x &lt; 1}`, `Correct. There is no natural number less than 1 (lecture uses N = {1, 2, 3, …}).`], [`{∅}`, `It contains one element, ∅, so it is not empty.`], [`{0}`, `It contains the element 0, so it is not empty.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `Let A = {a, b, c}. Which statement is FALSE?`,
      o: [[`∅ ⊆ A`, `True: the empty set is a subset of every set.`], [`{a, c} ⊆ A`, `This statement is true, so it is not the answer: a and c are both elements of A, so {a, c} ⊆ A.`], [`{a} ∈ A`, `Correct (it is false). {a} is a set; the elements of A are a, b, c, so {a} ⊆ A but {a} ∉ A.`], [`A ⊆ A`, `This statement is true, so it is not the answer: every element of A is in A, so every set is a subset of itself.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `How many elements does P(P(∅)) have?`,
      o: [[`0`, `A power set always contains ∅, so it is never empty.`], [`1`, `1 is |P(∅)|, since P(∅) = {∅}. Taking the power set again gives P({∅}) = {∅, {∅}}, with 2¹ = 2 elements.`], [`4`, `4 = 2² would need P(∅) to have 2 elements, but P(∅) = {∅} has only 1. So P(P(∅)) has 2¹ = 2 elements.`], [`2`, `Correct. P(∅) = {∅} has 1 element, so P(P(∅)) = {∅, {∅}} has 2¹ = 2.`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `Which pair of sets is equal?`,
      o: [[`{1, 2, 2, 3} and {3, 1, 2}`, `Correct. Repetition and order do not matter; both are {1, 2, 3}.`], [`{x | x is even, 0 &lt; x &lt; 6} and {2, 4, 6}`, `6 is not less than 6, so the first set is {2, 4}.`], [`{0} and ∅`, `{0} has one element (the number 0), while ∅ has no elements, so they are not equal. n({0}) = 1 and n(∅) = 0.`], [`{1, 2} and {{1}, {2}}`, `The second set's elements are sets, not numbers.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `Which set is infinite?`,
      o: [[`{x : x ∈ N, x &lt; 7}`, `With N = {1, 2, 3, …}, this is {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}, a definite number of elements. The lecture lists it as a finite set.`], [`{x : x ∈ N, x &gt; 1}`, `Correct. A lecture example of an infinite set: 2, 3, 4, … never ends.`], [`The prime numbers up to 97`, `The primes up to 97 stop at 97, so there is a definite count (25). The lecture gives this as a finite set; all primes would be infinite.`], [`The colours of the rainbow`, `Finite (7 colours), a lecture example of a finite set.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `If A = {1, 2, 3} and B = {2, 3, 4}, which set can be taken as a universal set for both?`,
      o: [[`{1, 2, 3}`, `{1, 2, 3} is A itself, and it does not contain 4 ∈ B. A universal set must contain all elements of every given set.`], [`{2, 3}`, `{2, 3} is A ∩ B, the common elements only. It misses 1 ∈ A and 4 ∈ B, so it cannot be universal.`], [`{1, 2, 3, 4}`, `Correct. It contains every element of A and B.`], [`{1, 4}`, `{1, 4} misses 2 and 3, which belong to both A and B. A universal set must contain every element of A and B: {1, 2, 3, 4}.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `How many proper subsets (subsets other than the set itself) does {p, q, r} have?`,
      o: [[`3`, `3 counts only the one-element subsets {p}, {q}, {r}. The proper subsets also include ∅ and the three pairs: 2³ − 1 = 7.`], [`8`, `8 = 2³ counts all subsets, including {p, q, r} itself, which the question excludes. Proper subsets: 8 − 1 = 7.`], [`6`, `This also removes ∅, but ∅ is a proper subset.`], [`7`, `Correct. The set has 2³ = 8 subsets (L1, Power set); removing the set itself leaves 8 − 1 = 7: ∅, {p}, {q}, {r}, {p, q}, {p, r}, {q, r}.`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `Which notation correctly writes "the set of odd numbers between 0 and 100" in set-builder form?`,
      o: [[`{x | x is odd and 0 &lt; x &lt; 100}`, `Correct. This is the lecture's example of the property method.`], [`{x | x &lt; 100}`, `{x | x &lt; 100} drops the condition "x is odd" and the lower bound 0, so it contains even numbers and negatives as well.`], [`x ∈ odd`, `"x ∈ odd" is just a statement about x, not a set: set-builder form needs braces and a property, {x | property}.`], [`{1, 3, 5}`, `{1, 3, 5} is the listing (roster) method and stops after three elements; it is not set-builder form and misses 7, 9, …, 99.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `Let C = {2⁰, 2¹, 2², 2³, 2⁴}. Which statement is true?`,
      o: [[`9 ∈ C`, `C = {2⁰, …, 2⁴} = {1, 2, 4, 8, 16}, and 9 is not a power of 2, so 9 ∉ C (L1, Example 1.1).`], [`8 ∈ C`, `Correct. C = {2⁰, 2¹, 2², 2³, 2⁴} = {1, 2, 4, 8, 16}, and 8 = 2³ is listed, so 8 ∈ C (L1, Example 1.1).`], [`n(C) = 4`, `The exponents run 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, which is five powers, so n(C) = 5. Counting 1 to 4 forgets 2⁰.`], [`0 ∈ C`, `2⁰ = 1, not 0, so 0 is not in C = {1, 2, 4, 8, 16}. Any number to the power 0 equals 1.`]],
      a: 1 }
  ],
  2: [
    { q: `U = {1, …, 8}, A = {1, 2, 5}, B = {2, 3, 5, 7}. Find Aᶜ ∩ B.`,
      o: [[`{4, 6, 8}`, `{4, 6, 8} are the elements outside both A and B, (A ∪ B)ᶜ = Aᶜ ∩ Bᶜ. Aᶜ ∩ B must keep elements of B, giving {3, 7}.`], [`{3, 7}`, `Correct. Aᶜ = {3, 4, 6, 7, 8}; intersecting with B gives {3, 7} (= B − A).`], [`{2, 5}`, `{2, 5} is A ∩ B, the elements in both. Aᶜ ∩ B keeps elements of B that are outside A: {3, 7}.`], [`{1}`, `{1} is A − B (in A, not in B). Aᶜ ∩ B is the reverse, B − A = {3, 7}.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `With the same sets (U = {1..8}, A = {1, 2, 5}, B = {2, 3, 5, 7}), Aᶜ ∩ Bᶜ =`,
      o: [[`∅`, `∅ would mean every element of U is in A or B, but 4, 6 and 8 are in neither. Aᶜ ∩ Bᶜ = (A ∪ B)ᶜ = {4, 6, 8}.`], [`{1, 3, 4, 6, 7, 8}`, `{1, 3, 4, 6, 7, 8} = U − {2, 5} is (A ∩ B)ᶜ = Aᶜ ∪ Bᶜ. With ∩ the answer is (A ∪ B)ᶜ = {4, 6, 8}.`], [`{4, 6, 8}`, `Correct. A ∪ B = {1, 2, 3, 5, 7}, so (A ∪ B)ᶜ = {4, 6, 8} (De Morgan).`], [`{3, 7}`, `{3, 7} is Aᶜ ∩ B = B − A. Intersecting with Bᶜ instead removes B's elements too, leaving {4, 6, 8}.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `Which identity is the Absorption law?`,
      o: [[`(Aᶜ)ᶜ = A`, `(Aᶜ)ᶜ = A is the complementation law: complementing twice gives A back. Absorption involves two sets, A ∩ (A ∪ B) = A.`], [`A ∪ A = A`, `A ∪ A = A is the idempotent law (one set combined with itself). Absorption combines A with an expression containing B.`], [`A ∪ ∅ = A`, `A ∪ ∅ = A is the identity law: ∅ is the identity for union. Absorption is A ∩ (A ∪ B) = A or A ∪ (A ∩ B) = A.`], [`A ∩ (A ∪ B) = A`, `Correct. Absorption laws: A ∩ (A ∪ B) = A and A ∪ (A ∩ B) = A. A is inside A ∪ B, so intersecting with it leaves A (L2, Set identities).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `A = {a, b}, B = {1, 2, 3}. How many ordered pairs are in A × B?`,
      o: [[`6`, `Correct. Each of the 2 elements of A pairs with each of the 3 elements of B, so |A × B| = |A|·|B| = 2 × 3 = 6 (L2, Cartesian product).`], [`9`, `9 = 3² treats both sets as having 3 elements, i.e. B × B. Here |A| = 2, so |A × B| = 2 × 3 = 6.`], [`8`, `8 = 2³ is a power, like counting subsets. The Cartesian product multiplies: |A × B| = 2 × 3 = 6.`], [`5`, `5 = 2 + 3 adds the sizes, which would be |A ∪ B| for disjoint sets. Ordered pairs multiply: 2 × 3 = 6.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `If A ⊆ B, then A ∪ B equals:`,
      o: [[`∅`, `A ∪ B = ∅ only when both sets are empty. In general it contains at least all of B, and since A ⊆ B it equals B.`], [`B`, `Correct. Every element of A is already in B, so uniting adds nothing new: if A ⊆ B then A ∪ B = B (and A ∩ B = A) (L2, More examples).`], [`U`, `A ∪ B = U only if B happens to be the whole universal set. Since A ⊆ B, the union is just B.`], [`A`, `A is what you get for A ∩ B when A ⊆ B. The union keeps all of B, so A ∪ B = B.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `Which law gives A ∩ (B ∪ C) = (A ∩ B) ∪ (A ∩ C)?`,
      o: [[`Associative`, `Associative regroups brackets with the same operation, e.g. A ∩ (B ∩ C) = (A ∩ B) ∩ C. Here ∩ is spread over ∪.`], [`De Morgan`, `De Morgan involves complements: (A ∪ B)ᶜ = Aᶜ ∩ Bᶜ. This identity has no complements.`], [`Distributive`, `Correct. The distributive law spreads ∩ over ∪: A ∩ (B ∪ C) = (A ∩ B) ∪ (A ∩ C), like multiplying out brackets (L2, Set identities).`], [`Commutative`, `Commutative only swaps order, e.g. A ∩ B = B ∩ A. Here A is distributed into the bracket, which is the distributive law.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `U = {1, …, 6}, A = {1, 2, 3}, B = {3, 4}. (A ∩ B)ᶜ =`,
      o: [[`{5, 6}`, `{5, 6} removes all of A ∪ B = {1, 2, 3, 4}, which gives (A ∪ B)ᶜ. For (A ∩ B)ᶜ only the common element 3 is removed.`], [`{3}`, `{3} is A ∩ B itself, before complementing. Its complement is everything else in U: {1, 2, 4, 5, 6}.`], [`{1, 2}`, `{1, 2} is A − B. The complement of A ∩ B = {3} contains all of U except 3: {1, 2, 4, 5, 6}.`], [`{1, 2, 4, 5, 6}`, `Correct. A ∩ B = {3}, so (A ∩ B)ᶜ = U − {3} = {1, 2, 4, 5, 6}, which also equals Aᶜ ∪ Bᶜ by De Morgan (L2, Set identities).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `A ∩ Aᶜ =`,
      o: [[`∅`, `Correct. Complement law: no element is both in A and not in A.`], [`U`, `U is A ∪ Aᶜ (every element is either in A or not). The intersection A ∩ Aᶜ is ∅ by the complement law.`], [`A`, `A would need elements that are in A and also in Aᶜ, but Aᶜ holds only elements outside A. The complement law gives ∅.`], [`Aᶜ`, `Aᶜ ∩ Aᶜ = Aᶜ (idempotent), but intersecting with A removes everything in Aᶜ. Complement law: A ∩ Aᶜ = ∅.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `A = {1, 2, 3, 4}, B = {3, 4, 5}. B − A =`,
      o: [[`{3, 4}`, `{3, 4} is A ∩ B, the common elements. B − A removes exactly those from B, leaving {5}.`], [`{5}`, `Correct. B − A = {x | x ∈ B and x ∉ A}: from {3, 4, 5} remove 3 and 4 (they are in A), leaving {5} (L2, Difference).`], [`{1, 2, 5}`, `{1, 2, 5} is the symmetric difference (A − B) ∪ (B − A). B − A keeps only B's side: {5}.`], [`{1, 2}`, `{1, 2} is A − B, the elements of A not in B. Difference is not symmetric; B − A = {5}.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `In the Venn diagram of Aᶜ, the shaded region is:`,
      o: [[`The overlap of A and B`, `The overlap of the two circles is shaded for A ∩ B. Aᶜ shades everything in U that is not inside circle A.`], [`Everything outside the rectangle`, `The rectangle is the universal set U and nothing lies outside it. Aᶜ is shaded inside the rectangle, outside circle A.`], [`Everything inside the rectangle U but outside circle A`, `Correct. Aᶜ = {x : x ∈ U but x ∉ A} = U − A, so the Venn diagram shades the rectangle U except the circle A (L2, Complement).`], [`The inside of circle A`, `Shading the inside of circle A shows A itself. The complement is the opposite region: inside U, outside A.`]],
      a: 2 }
  ],
  3: [
    { q: `If p is true and q is false, what is the value of (p ∨ q) ∧ ¬q?`,
      o: [[`Depends on r`, `The formula uses only p and q; there is no r, so its value is fixed by p = T and q = F: it is T.`], [`False`, `Both parts are true: p ∨ q = T ∨ F = T and ¬q = ¬F = T, so the conjunction is T, not F.`], [`True`, `Correct. p ∨ q = T ∨ F = T (one true part is enough) and ¬q = T, so (p ∨ q) ∧ ¬q = T ∧ T = T (L3, Truth tables of the connectives).`], [`Undefined`, `Every compound proposition has a value in every row of its truth table. Here it works out to T ∧ T = T.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `How many rows does the truth table of a formula with 4 variables have?`,
      o: [[`12`, `12 = 3 × 4 is not a power of 2. Each variable doubles the rows: 2⁴ = 16.`], [`8`, `8 = 2³ is the row count for 3 variables. A fourth variable doubles it to 2⁴ = 16.`], [`4`, `4 = 2² is the row count for 2 variables (or 4 mistaken for the number of rows). With 4 variables: 2⁴ = 16.`], [`16`, `Correct. A truth table with n variables has 2ⁿ rows (each variable T or F): 2⁴ = 16 (L3, Vocabulary).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `p ⊕ q has the same truth table as:`,
      o: [[`¬(p ↔ q)`, `Correct. ⊕ is true exactly when p and q differ.`], [`p ↔ q`, `p ↔ q is true when p and q have equal values; ⊕ is true when they differ. Their columns are exact opposites, so ⊕ ≡ ¬(p ↔ q).`], [`p ∨ q`, `p ∨ q agrees with ⊕ except in row TT: there ∨ is T but ⊕ is F.`], [`p ∧ q`, `p ∧ q is T only in row TT, which is exactly where ⊕ is F; in rows TF and FT they also disagree.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `p: "It is raining", q: "I take an umbrella". Which situation makes p → q false?`,
      o: [[`It is raining and I take an umbrella`, `Raining and taking an umbrella is T → T, which is true: the promise was kept.`], [`It is raining and I do not take an umbrella`, `Correct. p → q is false only when the antecedent is true and the consequent false: it rains (T) and I take no umbrella (F) (L3, Connectives).`], [`It is not raining and I do not take an umbrella`, `Not raining makes the antecedent false, so F → F is true; the statement says nothing about dry days.`], [`It is not raining and I take an umbrella`, `Not raining makes the antecedent false, so F → T is true. Taking an umbrella anyway does not break the promise.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `Which sentence is a proposition?`,
      o: [[`Is 7 prime?`, `"Is 7 prime?" is a question; it states no fact, so it has no truth value and is not a proposition.`], [`x + 5 = 7`, `Its truth depends on x (true only for x = 2). Like the lecture's x + 1 = 2, it is not a proposition until x is given.`], [`5 + 3 = 9`, `Correct. It is a false statement, but still a proposition.`], [`Close the door.`, `"Close the door." is a command; it cannot be true or false, so it is not a proposition.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `The negation of "Today is Thursday" is:`,
      o: [[`Today is Thursday or Friday`, `This is true on Thursday too, so it cannot be the negation. ¬p must have the opposite truth value of p every day.`], [`Tomorrow is Thursday`, `"Tomorrow is Thursday" is a different proposition about another day. The negation is "it is not the case that today is Thursday".`], [`Today is Friday`, `"Today is Friday" is only one way p can be false; on Monday both p and this statement are false. The negation is "Today is not Thursday".`], [`Today is not Thursday`, `Correct. ¬p means "it is not the case that p" and has the opposite truth value; the lecture's example gives ¬p: "Today is not Thursday" (L3, Propositions and negation).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `For which values is p ↔ q true?`,
      o: [[`p = F, q = F`, `Correct. p ↔ q is true when p and q have equal truth values, both true or both false; F ↔ F = T (L3, Connectives).`], [`Never`, `p ↔ q is true in two rows, TT and FF, whenever the values are equal, so "never" is wrong.`], [`p = T, q = F`, `The values differ (T and F), so p ↔ q = F. The biconditional needs equal truth values.`], [`p = F, q = T`, `The values differ (F and T), so p ↔ q = F. Unlike p → q, the biconditional is false here.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `In the truth table of ¬P ∧ (P → Q), how many rows are true?`,
      o: [[`1`, `There are two true rows, not one: P = F with Q = T and P = F with Q = F both give ¬P = T and P → Q = T.`], [`2`, `Correct. The expression is T exactly when P = F (then ¬P = T and P → Q = T), which happens in 2 of the 4 rows (L3, Example ¬P ∧ (P → Q)).`], [`3`, `When P = T, ¬P = F and the conjunction is F, so rows TT and TF are false. Only 2 rows are true.`], [`4`, `4 true rows would make it a tautology, but the two rows with P = T give F.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `The negation of "Some students passed" is:`,
      o: [[`Some students did not pass`, `"Some did not pass" can be true together with "some passed" (a mixed class), so it does not deny it.`], [`Some students passed and some failed`, `This adds to the original instead of denying it; it is true only when the original is also true.`], [`No student passed`, `Correct. ¬(∃x P(x)) ≡ ∀x ¬P(x): denying that some student passed means every student did not pass, i.e. no student passed (L3, Negating quantifiers).`], [`All students passed`, `"All passed" makes "some passed" true as well, so it cannot be its negation.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `If p → q is FALSE, what are the truth values of p and q?`,
      o: [[`p = T, q = T`, `T → T is true, since the consequent holds. p → q is false only for p = T, q = F.`], [`p = F, q = F`, `F → F is true because a false antecedent makes the implication true.`], [`p = F, q = T`, `F → T is true: the antecedent is false. The only false row is p = T, q = F.`], [`p = T, q = F`, `Correct. An implication is false only when the antecedent is true and the consequent false, so p = T and q = F (L3, Connectives).`]],
      a: 3 }
  ],
  4: [
    { q: `Which law justifies the step (p ∨ ¬q) ∧ (p ∨ q) ≡ p ∨ (¬q ∧ q)?`,
      o: [[`De Morgan`, `De Morgan pushes a negation into a bracket. In this step no bracket is negated; p is factored out of two disjunctions.`], [`Associative`, `The associative law only regroups one connective, (p ∨ q) ∨ r ≡ p ∨ (q ∨ r). Here ∨ and ∧ swap places around p.`], [`Absorption`, `Absorption removes a term, p ∧ (p ∨ q) ≡ p. Here nothing disappears; p ∨ is factored out of both brackets.`], [`Distributive`, `Correct. p ∨ (x ∧ y) ≡ (p ∨ x) ∧ (p ∨ y), read right to left.`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `Simplify p ∧ (p ∨ q) ∧ T.`,
      o: [[`p`, `Correct. Absorption gives p, then identity p ∧ T ≡ p.`], [`p ∨ q`, `Absorption shrinks p ∧ (p ∨ q) to p, so p ∨ q is too big: at p = F, q = T it is T but the expression is F.`], [`q`, `q is absorbed: p ∧ (p ∨ q) ≡ p. At p = F, q = T the expression is F while q is T.`], [`T`, `The expression is F whenever p = F (a conjunction with p), so it is not a tautology. It simplifies to p.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `Which statement form is a contradiction?`,
      o: [[`p → p`, `p → p is false only for T → F, which cannot happen with the same p, so it is always true, a tautology.`], [`(p ∧ ¬p) ∧ q`, `Correct. p ∧ ¬p ≡ c by the negation law, and c ∧ q ≡ c by the universal bound law, so the form is false in every row (L4, Theorem 1.1.1).`], [`(p ∧ q) → p`, `(p ∧ q) → p is a tautology: if p ∧ q is T then p is T, so the implication can never be T → F.`], [`p ∨ ¬p`, `p ∨ ¬p is the standard tautology (negation law p ∨ ~p ≡ t), always true, the opposite of a contradiction.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `The equivalence p ∨ c ≡ p (c = contradiction) is called the:`,
      o: [[`Negation law`, `The negation laws are p ∨ ~p ≡ t and p ∧ ~p ≡ c; they involve ~p. p ∨ c ≡ p has no negation.`], [`Universal bound law`, `The universal bound laws are p ∨ t ≡ t and p ∧ c ≡ c, where the result is the constant. Here the result is p.`], [`Identity law`, `Correct. Identity laws p ∧ t ≡ p and p ∨ c ≡ p: "or false" leaves p unchanged (Theorem 1.1.1, law 4) (L4).`], [`Idempotent law`, `The idempotent law combines p with itself, p ∨ p ≡ p. Here p is combined with c, which is the identity law.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `To prove that two statement forms are NOT logically equivalent, you should:`,
      o: [[`Count their variables`, `Having different variable counts does not decide equivalence (p ∨ (p ∧ q) ≡ p). You must compare truth values in a row.`], [`Show that both are tautologies`, `If both were tautologies their columns would be all T and they would be equivalent, the opposite of what you want to show.`], [`Apply the laws of Theorem 1.1.1 until they differ`, `The lecture says the laws cannot prove non-equivalence.`], [`Find one row of the truth table where their values differ`, `Correct. Equivalence means the columns agree in every row, so one row where they differ proves non-equivalence; the laws cannot show this (L4, Using truth tables vs laws).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `¬(p ∨ q ∨ r) is equivalent to:`,
      o: [[`¬p ∧ ¬q ∧ ¬r`, `Correct. De Morgan applied twice: ¬((p ∨ q) ∨ r) ≡ ¬(p ∨ q) ∧ ¬r ≡ ¬p ∧ ¬q ∧ ¬r. None of them is true (L4, Theorem 1.1.1 law 9).`], [`¬p ∨ ¬q ∨ ¬r`, `¬p ∨ ¬q ∨ ¬r is De Morgan for ¬(p ∧ q ∧ r). Negating a disjunction must turn every ∨ into ∧.`], [`¬p ∧ q ∧ r`, `This negates only p and also changes ∨ to ∧ for q and r. De Morgan negates every part: ¬p ∧ ¬q ∧ ¬r.`], [`p ∧ q ∧ r`, `p ∧ q ∧ r drops the negation entirely. De Morgan keeps it on every part: ¬p ∧ ¬q ∧ ¬r.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `Simplify ¬(¬p ∨ ¬q).`,
      o: [[`¬p ∧ ¬q`, `De Morgan gives ¬(¬p) ∧ ¬(¬q); the double negations must then cancel to p and q, not stay as ¬p ∧ ¬q.`], [`p ∧ q`, `Correct. De Morgan: ¬(¬p ∨ ¬q) ≡ ¬(¬p) ∧ ¬(¬q); double negative law gives p ∧ q (L4, Theorem 1.1.1 laws 9 and 6).`], [`p ∨ q`, `De Morgan flips the ∨ inside to ∧ when the negation moves in, so the result is p ∧ q, not p ∨ q.`], [`T`, `¬(¬p ∨ ¬q) ≡ p ∧ q is F whenever p or q is F, so it is not a tautology.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `p ≡ q (logical equivalence) means:`,
      o: [[`p and q have the same variables`, `Sharing variables does not make forms equivalent (p ∧ q and p ∨ q have the same variables). Their columns must agree.`], [`p ∧ q is a tautology`, `p ∧ q being a tautology would mean both are always true. Equivalence only needs equal values in every row, i.e. p ↔ q always true.`], [`p ↔ q is a tautology`, `Correct. The lecture defines p ≡ q to mean p ↔ q is a tautology, i.e. p and q have the same truth value in every row (L4, Definitions).`], [`p → q is true in one row`, `One true row of p → q says little; equivalence needs agreement in every row, i.e. p ↔ q true in all rows.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `Which pair is NOT logically equivalent?`,
      o: [[`p ∧ q and q ∧ p`, `Commutative law p ∧ q ≡ q ∧ p, so this pair is equivalent and not the answer.`], [`p ∨ (p ∧ q) and p`, `Absorption law p ∨ (p ∧ q) ≡ p, so this pair is equivalent and not the answer.`], [`¬(¬p) and p`, `Double negative law ~(~p) ≡ p, so this pair is equivalent and not the answer.`], [`p → q and q → p`, `Correct. They differ when p = T, q = F (first F, second T).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `In Theorem 1.1.1, ~t ≡ c and ~c ≡ t are called:`,
      o: [[`Negations of t and c`, `Correct. Law 11 of Theorem 1.1.1 is "negations of t and c": the negation of a tautology is a contradiction (~t ≡ c) and vice versa (L4).`], [`Identity laws`, `The identity laws are p ∧ t ≡ p and p ∨ c ≡ p (law 4); they combine p with a constant, without negation.`], [`Double negative law`, `The double negative law is ~(~p) ≡ p (law 6), two negations of a variable. ~t ≡ c is one negation of a constant.`], [`Universal bound laws`, `The universal bound laws are p ∨ t ≡ t and p ∧ c ≡ c (law 8). ~t ≡ c is law 11, negations of t and c.`]],
      a: 0 }
  ],
  5: [
    { q: `The negation of "If it snows, then school is closed" is:`,
      o: [[`It snows and school is not closed`, `Correct. ¬(p → q) ≡ p ∧ ¬q: keep the hypothesis "it snows" and negate the conclusion, joined by "and" (L5, Negation of a conditional).`], [`If school is closed, then it snows`, `"If school is closed, then it snows" is the converse q → p. The negation of an if-then is an "and" statement, not another if-then.`], [`If it does not snow, then school is open`, `"If it does not snow, then school is open" is the inverse ¬p → ¬q, still an if-then. The negation is p ∧ ¬q.`], [`It does not snow and school is closed`, `¬p ∧ q (no snow, school closed) is a case where the original is true (false hypothesis), so it cannot be the negation.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `The contrapositive of p → q is:`,
      o: [[`p ∧ ¬q`, `p ∧ ¬q is the negation of p → q, which has the opposite value in every row; the contrapositive is equivalent to it.`], [`¬q → ¬p`, `Correct. The contrapositive negates and swaps both parts: ¬q → ¬p, and it is logically equivalent to p → q (Table 7) (L5).`], [`¬p → ¬q`, `¬p → ¬q negates without swapping, which is the inverse; it is equivalent to the converse, not to p → q.`], [`q → p`, `q → p swaps without negating, which is the converse. The contrapositive does both: ¬q → ¬p.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `p ∨ q is equivalent to:`,
      o: [[`¬p → ¬q`, `¬p → ¬q ≡ p ∨ ¬q, which is T at p = F, q = F, where p ∨ q is F.`], [`p → q`, `p → q ≡ ¬p ∨ q, which is T at p = F, q = F, where p ∨ q is F.`], [`¬p → q`, `Correct. ¬p → q ≡ ¬(¬p) ∨ q ≡ p ∨ q: "if not p then q" means at least one is true (L5, Table 7).`], [`q → p`, `q → p ≡ ¬q ∨ p, which is T at p = F, q = F, where p ∨ q is F.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `Using the order of operations, ¬p ∨ q → r means:`,
      o: [[`¬(p ∨ q) → r`, `Negation has the highest priority and applies only to the symbol right after it, p, not to the whole p ∨ q.`], [`¬(p ∨ (q → r))`, `This puts → inside and ¬ around everything, but ¬ binds only p and → is evaluated last.`], [`¬p ∨ (q → r)`, `This evaluates → before ∨, but → has priority 3 and ∨ priority 2, so ∨ is grouped first.`], [`(¬p ∨ q) → r`, `Correct. Order of operations: ~ first (¬p), then ∨ (¬p ∨ q), then → last, giving (¬p ∨ q) → r (L5, Order of operations).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `If p = T, q = F, r = T, what is (p → q) ∨ (p → r)?`,
      o: [[`T`, `Correct. p → q = F, p → r = T; F ∨ T = T. Also equals p → (q ∨ r) = T → T.`], [`Undefined`, `Every compound proposition has a value for given truth values: p → q = F, p → r = T, so the result is T.`], [`Depends on s`, `There is no s in the formula; its value is fixed by p, q and r: F ∨ T = T.`], [`F`, `p → r = T → T = T, and a disjunction with a true part is true. Only p → q is F.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `(p ∧ q) → r is equivalent to:`,
      o: [[`p → (q ∧ r)`, `p → (q ∧ r) is F at p = T, q = F, r = F, but (p ∧ q) → r = F → F = T there.`], [`(p → r) ∨ (q → r)`, `Correct. Table 7: (p → r) ∨ (q → r) ≡ (p ∧ q) → r. Both are false only when p = q = T and r = F (L5).`], [`(p → r) ∧ (q → r)`, `(p → r) ∧ (q → r) ≡ (p ∨ q) → r (division into cases). It is F at p = T, q = F, r = F, where (p ∧ q) → r is T.`], [`r → (p ∧ q)`, `r → (p ∧ q) is the converse; at r = T, p = F it is F while (p ∧ q) → r is T.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `Which is a correct step in proving ¬(p ∨ (¬p ∧ q)) ≡ ¬p ∧ ¬q?`,
      o: [[`¬(p ∨ (¬p ∧ q)) ≡ ¬p ∧ (¬p ∧ q)`, `De Morgan negates both parts: the second part must become ¬(¬p ∧ q), not stay (¬p ∧ q).`], [`¬(p ∨ (¬p ∧ q)) ≡ ¬p ∨ ¬(¬p ∧ q)`, `De Morgan on ¬(a ∨ b) gives ¬a ∧ ¬b; the ∨ must change to ∧, so ¬p ∨ … is wrong.`], [`¬(p ∨ (¬p ∧ q)) ≡ ¬p ∧ ¬(¬p ∧ q)`, `Correct. The second De Morgan law ¬(a ∨ b) ≡ ¬a ∧ ¬b with a = p, b = ¬p ∧ q gives ¬p ∧ ¬(¬p ∧ q), the first step of the lecture's proof (L5).`], [`¬(p ∨ (¬p ∧ q)) ≡ p ∧ (¬p ∧ q)`, `The negation has disappeared from both parts; De Morgan needs ¬p and ¬(¬p ∧ q) joined by ∧.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `Which formula is a tautology (modus ponens)?`,
      o: [[`[q ∧ (p → q)] → p`, `False when p = F, q = T (affirming the consequent).`], [`(p → q) → (q → p)`, `(p → q) → (q → p) is false at p = F, q = T: p → q = T but its converse q → p = F, so it is not a tautology.`], [`p → (p ∧ q)`, `p → (p ∧ q) is false at p = T, q = F: p is true but p ∧ q is false.`], [`[p ∧ (p → q)] → q`, `Correct. Whenever p and p → q are true, q is true.`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `¬(p ↔ q) is equivalent to:`,
      o: [[`p ↔ ¬q`, `Correct. ¬(p ↔ q) is true when p and q differ, i.e. when p equals ¬q: ¬(p ↔ q) ≡ p ↔ ¬q (L5, Table 8).`], [`p ∧ q`, `p ∧ q is T at p = q = T, where p ↔ q is T and so its negation is F.`], [`¬p ↔ ¬q`, `¬p ↔ ¬q ≡ p ↔ q (Table 8): negating both sides keeps them equal, so it is the biconditional, not its negation.`], [`(p → q) ∧ (q → p)`, `(p → q) ∧ (q → p) ≡ p ↔ q (the definition), the opposite of ¬(p ↔ q).`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `The negation of "John is rich or famous" is:`,
      o: [[`If John is rich, he is famous`, `R → F is true when John is neither rich nor famous, and also when he is not rich but famous, where the original is true. Not a negation.`], [`John is neither rich nor famous`, `Correct. De Morgan: ¬(R ∨ F) ≡ ¬R ∧ ¬F, i.e. "John is not rich and not famous" = neither rich nor famous (L4, De Morgan; L5).`], [`John is not rich or not famous`, `¬R ∨ ¬F is De Morgan's negation of "rich and famous" (R ∧ F). Negating "or" must give "and".`], [`John is rich and not famous`, `R ∧ ¬F (rich, not famous) makes the original R ∨ F true, so it cannot be the negation.`]],
      a: 1 }
  ],
  6: [
    { q: `A coin is tossed 3 times; X = number of heads. f(2) =`,
      o: [[`2/8`, `2/8 misses one outcome: HHT, HTH and THH all have exactly two heads, so f(2) = 3/8.`], [`3/8`, `Correct. 3 tosses give n(S) = 2³ = 8 outcomes, and exactly two heads occur in HHT, HTH, THH: f(2) = 3/8 (L6, Random variable).`], [`1/2`, `1/2 = 4/8, but only 3 of the 8 outcomes have exactly 2 heads.`], [`1/8`, `1/8 is f(0) (TTT) or f(3) (HHH), a single outcome. Two heads occur in three outcomes: 3/8.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `Two dice are tossed; X = sum. P(X = 8) =`,
      o: [[`4/36`, `4/36 is P(X = 5) or P(X = 9), each with 4 pairs. Sum 8 has 5 pairs: (2,6), (3,5), (4,4), (5,3), (6,2).`], [`6/36`, `6/36 is P(X = 7), the most likely sum. Sum 8 has one pair fewer: 5/36.`], [`5/36`, `Correct. Of the 36 outcomes, the sum 8 comes from (2,6), (3,5), (4,4), (5,3), (6,2), so P(X = 8) = 5/36 (L6, Example 2).`], [`8/36`, `No sum has 8 pairs; the largest count is 6, for sum 7. Listing the pairs for 8 gives only 5.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `Two dice; X = the greater of the two numbers. f(3) =`,
      o: [[`9/36`, `9/36 = 3²/36 counts pairs with both numbers at most 3, i.e. P(X ≤ 3). Subtract the 2² = 4 pairs with max at most 2: 5/36.`], [`6/36`, `6/36 counts (3, 3) twice, as (1,3), (2,3), (3,3) plus (3,1), (3,2), (3,3). There are only 5 distinct pairs.`], [`3/36`, `3/36 is f(2): (1,2), (2,1), (2,2). For a maximum of 3 there are 2·3 − 1 = 5 pairs.`], [`5/36`, `Correct. 3² − 2² = 5 pairs: (1,3), (2,3), (3,3), (3,2), (3,1).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `Which table can be a probability distribution?`,
      o: [[`f(1) = 0.2, f(2) = 0.3, f(3) = 0.5`, `Correct. A distribution needs f(x) ≥ 0 for every x and Σ f(x) = 1: here all values are non-negative and 0.2 + 0.3 + 0.5 = 1 (L6, Probability distribution function).`], [`f(1) = 0.3, f(2) = 0.3`, `The values are non-negative but 0.3 + 0.3 = 0.6 ≠ 1, so condition 2 (Σ f(x) = 1) fails.`], [`f(0) = 0.7, f(1) = −0.1, f(2) = 0.4`, `The sum is 0.7 − 0.1 + 0.4 = 1, but f(1) = −0.1 breaks condition 1, f(x) ≥ 0.`], [`f(0) = 0.5, f(1) = 0.6`, `0.5 + 0.6 = 1.1 &gt; 1, so condition 2 (Σ f(x) = 1) fails.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `Two cards are drawn without replacement from cards 1..4; X = the smaller number. f(1) =`,
      o: [[`2/12`, `2/12 is f(3): only (3, 4) and (4, 3). Any pair containing 1 has smaller number 1: 3 + 3 = 6 pairs.`], [`6/12`, `Correct. n(S) = 4 × 3 = 12; pairs containing 1: 3 + 3 = 6.`], [`4/12`, `4/12 is f(2): (2,3), (2,4) and their reverses. Pairs containing 1 number 6.`], [`3/12`, `3/12 counts (1,2), (1,3), (1,4) only. Draws are ordered, so (2,1), (3,1), (4,1) also count: 6/12.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `A coin is tossed twice; X = heads − tails. Range of X:`,
      o: [[`{0, 1, 2}`, `{0, 1, 2} is the range of the number of heads. Subtracting tails gives HH → 2, HT/TH → 0, TT → −2.`], [`{−2, −1, 0, 1, 2}`, `With 2 tosses, h − t = 2h − 2 is always even, so ±1 cannot occur. The range is {−2, 0, 2}.`], [`{−2, 0, 2}`, `Correct. HH → 2 − 0 = 2, HT and TH → 1 − 1 = 0, TT → 0 − 2 = −2, so the range is {−2, 0, 2} (L6, Try to solve).`], [`{−1, 0, 1}`, `±1 would need an odd number of tosses. With 2 tosses, h − t is even: {−2, 0, 2}.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `In X : S → R, the sample space S is the:`,
      o: [[`dependent variable`, `The dependent variable is the real number X produces (R). S holds the inputs, so it is the independent variable.`], [`co-domain`, `The co-domain of X : S → R is R, the set of real values. S is the domain.`], [`probability`, `Probabilities belong to the distribution f(x), not to S. S is the set of outcomes, the domain of X.`], [`independent variable (domain)`, `Correct. In X : S → R the sample space S is the domain (independent variable) and R the co-domain (dependent variable) (L6, Random variable).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `The number of outcomes when a die is tossed twice is:`,
      o: [[`36`, `Correct. Each toss has 6 outcomes and the two tosses are combined as ordered pairs: n(S) = 6 × 6 = 36 (L6, Example 2).`], [`11`, `11 is the number of possible sums (2 to 12), not outcomes. Ordered pairs give 6 × 6 = 36.`], [`6`, `6 is the number of outcomes of one toss. Two tosses give 6 × 6 = 36 ordered pairs.`], [`12`, `12 = 6 + 6 adds instead of multiplying. Each of 6 first results pairs with 6 second results: 36.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `X = number of heads in 3 tosses. P(X ≥ 2) =`,
      o: [[`7/8`, `7/8 = 1 − 1/8 is P(X ≥ 1), excluding only TTT. X ≥ 2 needs two or three heads: 3/8 + 1/8.`], [`4/8`, `Correct. P(X ≥ 2) = f(2) + f(3) = 3/8 + 1/8 = 4/8 (HHT, HTH, THH, HHH out of 8) (L6, Probability distribution).`], [`3/8`, `3/8 is only P(X = 2). X ≥ 2 also includes X = 3 (HHH): 3/8 + 1/8 = 4/8.`], [`1/8`, `1/8 is only P(X = 3) (HHH). Adding P(X = 2) = 3/8 gives 4/8.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `Measuring the exact time a bulb lasts gives a sample space that is:`,
      o: [[`discrete and finite`, `Time can take any value in an interval, so the outcomes cannot be counted; a discrete finite space has a definite number of outcomes.`], [`empty`, `The bulb lasts some amount of time, so there are outcomes, infinitely many of them. The space is not empty.`], [`continuous`, `Correct. Infinitely many possibilities like points on a line segment.`], [`discrete and countable`, `A discrete countable space is like the whole numbers. Exact times fill an interval like points on a segment, so it is continuous.`]],
      a: 2 }
  ],
  7: [
    { q: `X = number of heads in 3 fair coin tosses (f = 1/8, 3/8, 3/8, 1/8). E(X) =`,
      o: [[`1`, `1 is the mean for two tosses (L7, Example 1). For three: μ = 0(1/8) + 1(3/8) + 2(3/8) + 3(1/8) = 12/8 = 1.5.`], [`2`, `2 overweights the high values. Weighting each count by its probability gives (0 + 3 + 6 + 3)/8 = 1.5.`], [`1.5`, `Correct. μ = Σ x f(x) = 0·(1/8) + 1·(3/8) + 2·(3/8) + 3·(1/8) = (0 + 3 + 6 + 3)/8 = 12/8 = 1.5 (L7, Expectation).`], [`3`, `3 is the largest possible value (HHH), which has probability only 1/8. The weighted average is 12/8 = 1.5.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `For the same X (3 tosses), Var(X) =`,
      o: [[`3`, `3 is E(X²) = (0 + 3 + 12 + 9)/8. The variance subtracts μ² = 1.5² = 2.25, giving 0.75.`], [`2.25`, `2.25 is μ² = 1.5². The variance is E(X²) − μ² = 3 − 2.25 = 0.75.`], [`1.5`, `1.5 is the mean μ, not the variance. σ² = E(X²) − μ² = 3 − 2.25 = 0.75.`], [`0.75`, `Correct. E(X²) = (0 + 3 + 12 + 9)/8 = 3; 3 − 1.5² = 0.75.`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `x: 1, 2, 3 with f: 0.5, 0.3, 0.2. E(X) =`,
      o: [[`1.7`, `Correct. μ = Σ x f(x) = 1(0.5) + 2(0.3) + 3(0.2) = 0.5 + 0.6 + 0.6 = 1.7 (L7, Expectation).`], [`2`, `2 = (1 + 2 + 3)/3 treats the values as equally likely. Weighting by f gives 0.5 + 0.6 + 0.6 = 1.7.`], [`3.5`, `3.5 is E(X²) = 1(0.5) + 4(0.3) + 9(0.2). The mean uses x, not x²: 1.7.`], [`1.5`, `1.5 comes from multiplying 0.2 by 2 instead of 3 (0.5 + 0.6 + 0.4). The last term is 3 × 0.2 = 0.6, so μ = 1.7.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `For x: 1, 2, 3 with f: 0.5, 0.3, 0.2, σ² =`,
      o: [[`2.89`, `2.89 is μ² = 1.7². The variance is E(X²) − μ² = 3.5 − 2.89 = 0.61.`], [`0.61`, `Correct. E(X²) = 0.5 + 1.2 + 1.8 = 3.5; 3.5 − 1.7² = 0.61.`], [`3.5`, `3.5 is E(X²) = 0.5 + 1.2 + 1.8. Subtract μ² = 2.89 to get σ² = 0.61.`], [`1.7`, `1.7 is the mean μ. The variance is E(X²) − μ² = 3.5 − 2.89 = 0.61.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `x: −1, 1 with f: 0.5, 0.5. Mean and variance are:`,
      o: [[`μ = 1, σ² = 0`, `μ = 1 would need X = 1 always. Here μ = (−1)(0.5) + (1)(0.5) = 0, and the values sit 1 away from it, so σ² = 1.`], [`μ = 0.5, σ² = 0.5`, `0.5 is just the x = 1 term; adding (−1)(0.5) gives μ = 0. Then E(X²) = 1 and σ² = 1 − 0 = 1.`], [`μ = 0, σ² = 1`, `Correct. μ = (−1)(0.5) + (1)(0.5) = 0; E(X²) = 1(0.5) + 1(0.5) = 1; σ² = E(X²) − μ² = 1 − 0 = 1 (L7, Variance).`], [`μ = 0, σ² = 0`, `μ = 0 is right, but variance 0 means no spread; both values are at distance 1 from 0, so σ² = 1 − 0² = 1.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `x: 0, 1, 2 with f: 0.2, 0.5, a. Find a and E(X).`,
      o: [[`a = 0.3, E(X) = 0.8`, `a = 0.3 is right, but 0.8 = 0.5 + 0.3 forgets to multiply by x = 2: the last term is 2 × 0.3 = 0.6, so E(X) = 1.1.`], [`a = 0.5, E(X) = 1.5`, `With a = 0.5 the probabilities sum to 0.2 + 0.5 + 0.5 = 1.2 &gt; 1. Σ f = 1 forces a = 0.3.`], [`a = 0.2, E(X) = 0.9`, `With a = 0.2 the probabilities sum to 0.9, not 1. Σ f = 1 gives a = 0.3 and E(X) = 0.5 + 0.6 = 1.1.`], [`a = 0.3, E(X) = 1.1`, `Correct. Σ f = 1 gives a = 1 − 0.7 = 0.3; then E(X) = 0(0.2) + 1(0.5) + 2(0.3) = 0.5 + 0.6 = 1.1 (L7, Example 2 method).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `If σ² = 0.49, the standard deviation is:`,
      o: [[`0.7`, `Correct. σ is the positive square root of the variance: σ = √0.49 = 0.7, since 0.7² = 0.49 (L7, Variance and standard deviation).`], [`0.49`, `0.49 is the variance σ² itself. The standard deviation is its square root, 0.7.`], [`7`, `7 is √49: the decimal point was lost. Since 0.7² = 0.49, √0.49 = 0.7.`], [`0.2401`, `0.2401 = 0.49² squares the variance. σ is the square root: √0.49 = 0.7.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `A continuous X has f(x) = 1 on 0 &lt; x &lt; 1. E(X) =`,
      o: [[`1/12`, `1/12 is the variance, E(X²) − μ² = 1/3 − 1/4. The mean is ∫₀¹ x dx = 1/2.`], [`1/2`, `Correct. For continuous X, μ = ∫ x f(x) dx = ∫₀¹ x · 1 dx = [x²/2]₀¹ = 1/2 (L7, Expectation).`], [`1`, `1 is ∫₀¹ f(x) dx, the total probability. The mean multiplies by x first: ∫₀¹ x dx = 1/2.`], [`1/3`, `1/3 is E(X²) = ∫₀¹ x² dx. The mean uses x: ∫₀¹ x dx = 1/2.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `Which formula gives E(X²) for a discrete X?`,
      o: [[`Σ f(x)²`, `Σ f(x)² squares the probabilities, not the values. By the theorem E[g(X)] = Σ g(x) f(x) with g(x) = x², E(X²) = Σ x² f(x).`], [`Σ x f(x²)`, `Σ x f(x²) evaluates f at x², which mixes up the roles. The value is squared and weighted by f(x): Σ x² f(x).`], [`Σ x² f(x)`, `Correct. The theorem E[g(X)] = Σ g(x) f(x) with g(x) = x² gives E(X²) = Σ x² f(x) (L7, Expectation).`], [`(Σ x f(x))²`, `(Σ x f(x))² = μ², the square of the mean. In general E(X²) ≠ μ²; their difference is the variance.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `The expectation μ of a random variable can be described as:`,
      o: [[`the spread of the values`, `The spread of the values is measured by the variance σ² (or σ), not by the mean.`], [`the largest possible value`, `The mean is a weighted average and can be well below the largest value (e.g. 1.5 heads in 3 tosses, max 3).`], [`the number of outcomes`, `The number of outcomes is n(S), a count. The mean μ = Σ x f(x) is a weighted average of the values.`], [`the value at which most values are centralized (the mean)`, `Correct. The lecture defines expectation as the value at which most values of the random variable are centralized, also called the mean μ (L7, Expectation).`]],
      a: 3 }
  ],
  8: [
    { q: `−25 mod 7 =`,
      o: [[`5`, `5 is not the residue: −25 − 5 = −30 is not a multiple of 7. −25 = (−4)(7) + 3.`], [`−4`, `−4 is the computer's negative remainder (−25 = (−3)(7) − 4). Add the modulus to land in Z₇: −4 + 7 = 3.`], [`4`, `4 is 25 mod 7, which drops the minus sign. For −25 the remainder is −4 + 7 = 3.`], [`3`, `Correct. −25 = (−3)(7) − 4 gives r = −4; decrement q and add n: −25 = (−4)(7) + 3, so −25 mod 7 = 3 (L8, Example 2.3 method).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `gcd(84, 36) and the extended-Euclid values s, t (s·84 + t·36 = gcd) are:`,
      o: [[`gcd = 12, s = 1, t = −2`, `Correct. 84 = 2·36 + 12; 36 = 3·12 + 0; 84 − 2·36 = 12.`], [`gcd = 4, s = 1, t = −2`, `4 divides both 84 and 36 but is not the greatest; Euclid ends with remainder 12 (84 = 2·36 + 12, 36 = 3·12 + 0).`], [`gcd = 12, s = −2, t = 1`, `s and t are swapped: (−2)(84) + 1(36) = −168 + 36 = −132, not 12. The right pair is s = 1, t = −2.`], [`gcd = 6, s = 1, t = −2`, `With s = 1, t = −2 we get 84 − 72 = 12, not 6. Euclid gives gcd = 12 (36 = 3 × 12 exactly).`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `The multiplicative inverse of 7 in Z₂₆ is:`,
      o: [[`11`, `7 × 11 = 77 = 2 × 26 + 25 ≡ 25 (mod 26), not 1. (11's inverse is 19.)`], [`15`, `Correct. gcd(26, 7) = 1 so an inverse exists, and 7 × 15 = 105 = 4 × 26 + 1 ≡ 1 (mod 26) (L8, Inverses).`], [`None`, `b has an inverse in Zn when gcd(n, b) = 1, and gcd(26, 7) = 1, so 7 does have one: 15.`], [`19`, `7 × 19 = 133 = 5 × 26 + 3 ≡ 3, not 1. 19 is the inverse of 11 in Z₂₆.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `(15 × 17) mod 13 =`,
      o: [[`2`, `2 is only 15 mod 13; the product also needs 17 mod 13 = 4: (2 × 4) mod 13 = 8.`], [`5`, `5 does not match: 15 × 17 = 255 = 19 × 13 + 8. With the third property, (2 × 4) mod 13 = 8.`], [`8`, `Correct. 255 = 19 × 13 + 8 (or (2 × 4) mod 13 = 8).`], [`4`, `4 is only 17 mod 13. By the third property, multiply the residues: (2 × 4) mod 13 = 8.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `Which element of Z₁₂ has NO multiplicative inverse?`,
      o: [[`7`, `7 is its own inverse in Z₁₂: 7 × 7 = 49 = 4 × 12 + 1 ≡ 1, and gcd(12, 7) = 1.`], [`5`, `5 is its own inverse in Z₁₂: 5 × 5 = 25 = 2 × 12 + 1 ≡ 1, and gcd(12, 5) = 1.`], [`11`, `11 is its own inverse in Z₁₂: 11 × 11 = 121 = 10 × 12 + 1 ≡ 1, and gcd(12, 11) = 1.`], [`9`, `Correct. An inverse exists only if gcd(n, b) = 1; gcd(12, 9) = 3 ≠ 1, so 9 has no multiplicative inverse in Z₁₂ (L8, Inverses).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `By the third property, 10⁵ mod 9 =`,
      o: [[`1`, `Correct. Third property applied n times: 10⁵ mod 9 = (10 mod 9)⁵ mod 9 = 1⁵ = 1 (L8, Example 2.19).`], [`0`, `0 would mean 9 divides 100000, but its digit sum is 1. (10 mod 9)⁵ = 1⁵ = 1.`], [`5`, `5 is the exponent, not the residue. 10 mod 9 = 1, and 1⁵ = 1.`], [`10`, `10 is not in Z₉ = {0, …, 8}; 10 mod 9 = 1, and 1⁵ = 1.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `Which congruence is true?`,
      o: [[`5 ≡ 0 (mod 3)`, `5 mod 3 = 2 and 0 mod 3 = 0; the residues differ, so 5 ≢ 0 (mod 3).`], [`8 ≡ 13 (mod 5)`, `Correct. Both leave remainder 3 (lecture example).`], [`7 ≡ 2 (mod 3)`, `7 mod 3 = 1 but 2 mod 3 = 2, so they are not congruent; 7 − 2 = 5 is not a multiple of 3.`], [`13 ≡ 24 (mod 10)`, `13 mod 10 = 3 and 24 mod 10 = 4; the residues differ (24 − 13 = 11 is not a multiple of 10).`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `The additive inverse of 3 in Z₁₀ is:`,
      o: [[`1`, `3 + 1 = 4 ≢ 0 (mod 10). The additive inverse of a in Zn is n − a = 7.`], [`−3`, `−3 is the inverse in ordinary integers, but it is not in Z₁₀; mapped into Z₁₀ it is 10 − 3 = 7.`], [`7`, `Correct. a + b ≡ 0 (mod n) gives b = n − a = 10 − 3 = 7, and 3 + 7 = 10 ≡ 0; (3, 7) is a Z₁₀ pair (L8, Inverses).`], [`3`, `3 + 3 = 6 ≢ 0 (mod 10). The additive inverse is 10 − 3 = 7.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `−45 divided by 8 gives (with 0 ≤ r &lt; 8):`,
      o: [[`q = −5, r = 3`, `(−5)(8) + 3 = −37, not −45. The quotient must go one lower: (−6)(8) + 3 = −45.`], [`q = −5, r = −5`, `−45 = (−5)(8) − 5 is what a computer gives, but r must be non-negative: decrement q and add n, giving q = −6, r = 3.`], [`q = 5, r = 5`, `5 × 8 + 5 = 45, the division of +45. For −45 the quotient is negative: (−6)(8) + 3.`], [`q = −6, r = 3`, `Correct. −45 = (−5)(8) − 5 has r &lt; 0, so decrement q by 1 and add n to r: q = −6, r = −5 + 8 = 3 (L8, Example 2.3).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `gcd(1071, 462) =`,
      o: [[`21`, `Correct. 1071 = 2·462 + 147; 462 = 3·147 + 21; 147 = 7·21 + 0.`], [`147`, `147 is the first remainder (1071 − 2·462). It does not divide 462 (462 = 3·147 + 21), so Euclid continues to 21.`], [`7`, `7 divides both (1071 = 153·7, 462 = 66·7), but 21 also does, so 7 is not the greatest.`], [`42`, `42 divides 462 (= 11·42) but not 1071, which is odd. The last non-zero remainder is 21.`]],
      a: 0 }
  ],
  9: [
    { q: `A graph has 7 edges. The sum of the degrees of its vertices is:`,
      o: [[`14`, `Correct. Handshake proposition: Σ d(v) = 2|E| = 2 × 7 = 14, since each edge adds 1 to both of its endpoints (L9).`], [`7`, `7 counts each edge once, but each edge has two ends. Σ d(v) = 2 × 7 = 14.`], [`Depends on the number of vertices`, `Isolated vertices add 0 to the degree sum, so the number of vertices does not matter: Σ d(v) = 2|E| = 14.`], [`21`, `21 = 3 × 7 counts each edge three times. Each edge has exactly two endpoints: 2 × 7 = 14.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `Can a graph have exactly 3 vertices of odd degree?`,
      o: [[`Yes, if it is a multigraph`, `The handshake proposition holds for multigraphs too: each edge still adds 2 to the degree sum, so the odd-degree count stays even.`], [`No, the number of odd-degree vertices is always even`, `Correct (corollary of the handshake proposition).`], [`Only if it is complete`, `Completeness is irrelevant; the corollary holds for every graph. (In K_n all degrees are n − 1, so 0 or n vertices are odd.)`], [`Yes, if it has loops`, `A loop adds 2 to one vertex's degree, which does not change its parity, so the number of odd vertices is still even.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `How many edges does K₆ have?`,
      o: [[`36`, `36 = 6² counts all ordered pairs including (v, v). K₆ has one edge per unordered pair of distinct vertices: 6 × 5 / 2 = 15.`], [`30`, `30 = 6 × 5 counts each edge from both ends. Halving gives n(n − 1)/2 = 15.`], [`15`, `Correct. K_n has n(n − 1)/2 edges: 6 × 5 / 2 = 15 (each of the 6 vertices has degree 5) (L9, Special graphs).`], [`12`, `12 = 6 × 2 would be a 4-regular graph's edge count on 6 vertices. K₆ is 5-regular: 6 × 5 / 2 = 15.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `K_{3,3} is:`,
      o: [[`3-regular with 6 edges`, `K_{3,3} is 3-regular, but |E| = n₁ · n₂ = 3 × 3 = 9, not 6.`], [`2-regular with 6 edges`, `Each vertex in one part joins all 3 vertices of the other part, so the degree is 3, and |E| = 3 × 3 = 9.`], [`not bipartite`, `K_{3,3} is complete bipartite: its two parts of 3 vertices have no edges inside them, so it is bipartite.`], [`3-regular with 9 edges`, `Correct. |E| = 3 × 3; n₁ = n₂ so it is regular.`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `A walk in which no edge is repeated (vertices may repeat) is a:`,
      o: [[`trail`, `Correct. A trail is a walk with all different edges; its vertices may repeat (L9, Walks, trails, paths, cycles).`], [`path`, `A path requires all different nodes. Allowing repeated vertices with distinct edges is a trail.`], [`loop`, `A (self-)loop is a single edge from a vertex to itself, not a kind of walk.`], [`cycle`, `A cycle is closed (v₀ = v_k) with all other nodes different. The question allows repeated vertices and no closing condition.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `In a digraph with edges 1→2, 1→3, 3→2, 2→4, what is d_in(2)?`,
      o: [[`0`, `In-degree 0 would mean no edge points into 2, but 1→2 and 3→2 both end at 2.`], [`2`, `Correct. d_in(v) counts edges whose terminal is v: 1→2 and 3→2 both end at 2, so d_in(2) = 2 (L9, Directed graphs).`], [`1`, `1 counts only 1→2 and misses 3→2, which also ends at 2.`], [`3`, `2→4 starts at 2, so it counts toward d_out(2), not d_in(2). Only 1→2 and 3→2 enter 2.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `For the same digraph (1→2, 1→3, 3→2, 2→4), a topological order is:`,
      o: [[`4, 2, 3, 1`, `This is the reverse order: edge 1→2 needs 1 before 2, but here 1 comes last. Every edge would go backwards.`], [`1, 2, 3, 4`, `Edge 3→2 requires f(3) &lt; f(2), but this order puts 2 before 3.`], [`1, 3, 2, 4`, `Correct. Every edge goes forward in this order.`], [`2, 1, 3, 4`, `Edge 1→2 requires 1 before 2, but this order starts with 2. Only 1 has in-degree 0, so it must come first.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `The adjacency matrix of a simple undirected graph is always:`,
      o: [[`the identity matrix`, `The identity matrix has 1s only on the diagonal, which would mean self-loops; a simple graph has 0s there.`], [`full of ones`, `Only K_n has 1s in every off-diagonal place; other simple graphs have 0s for non-adjacent pairs, and the diagonal is always 0.`], [`n × m`, `n × m is the shape of the incidence matrix (vertices × edges). The adjacency matrix is n × n.`], [`symmetric with zeros on the diagonal`, `Correct. Edges are unordered and there are no loops.`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `In the directed incidence matrix for edge e = (v₂, v₅), the column of e has:`,
      o: [[`−1 at v₂ and 1 at v₅`, `Correct. Source −1, terminal +1 (lecture convention).`], [`1 at v₂ and −1 at v₅`, `These signs are reversed: the lecture puts −1 at the source v₂ and +1 at the terminal v₅.`], [`1 at v₂ and 1 at v₅`, `1 at both ends is the undirected incidence matrix. The directed version marks the source with −1.`], [`only one non-zero entry`, `An edge has two endpoints, so its column has two non-zero entries: −1 at v₂ and +1 at v₅.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `Which graph is NOT bipartite?`,
      o: [[`A cycle with 6 vertices`, `A 6-cycle is even, so colours can alternate around it (A, B, A, B, A, B) and every edge joins different colours: it is bipartite.`], [`A cycle with 5 vertices`, `Correct. Alternating 2 colours around a 5-cycle forces the last edge to join two vertices of the same colour; like the triangle, an odd cycle is not bipartite (L9, Special graphs).`], [`A path with 4 vertices`, `A path has no cycles, so alternate colours along it (A, B, A, B) separate every edge: bipartite.`], [`K_{2,3}`, `K_{2,3} is complete bipartite by definition: all its edges go between the part of 2 and the part of 3.`]],
      a: 1 }
  ],
  10: [
    { q: `A connected graph has 6 vertices and 9 edges. rank A(G) and rank B(G) are:`,
      o: [[`5 and 3`, `rank A = n − 1 = 5 is right, but rank B = m − n + 1 = 9 − 6 + 1 = 4, not 3.`], [`5 and 4`, `Correct. n − 1 = 5; m − n + 1 = 9 − 6 + 1 = 4.`], [`9 and 6`, `9 and 6 are just m and n. Rank A = n − 1 = 5 and rank B = m − n + 1 = 4.`], [`6 and 3`, `The rows of A sum to 0 mod 2, so rank A = n − 1 = 5, not 6; and rank B = 9 − 6 + 1 = 4.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `A graph with 8 vertices and 3 components has incidence-matrix rank:`,
      o: [[`8`, `8 is n, but the rows within each component are dependent. With k components rank A = n − k = 5.`], [`3`, `3 is k, the number of components. The rank is n − k = 8 − 3 = 5.`], [`5`, `Correct. Remark to Theorem 10.2: with k components rank A(G) = n − k, since each component loses one dependent row: 8 − 3 = 5 (L10).`], [`7`, `7 = n − 1 is the rank for a connected graph. With 3 components: n − k = 5.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `The sum of the entries in row v of the incidence matrix equals:`,
      o: [[`the number of edges of G`, `The total number of edges is m, the number of columns. A row counts only the edges at its vertex.`], [`2`, `2 is the sum of each column (every edge has two ends). A row sum is the degree of its vertex.`], [`n − 1`, `n − 1 is the rank of A for a connected graph. A row sum is that vertex's degree, which varies.`], [`the degree of v`, `Correct. The ones in row v mark the edges incident on v, so their number is the degree of v (L10, 10.1 Observation 2).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `The number of spanning trees of K₅ is:`,
      o: [[`125`, `Correct. Matrix-tree corollary: τ(K_n) = nⁿ⁻², so τ(K₅) = 5³ = 125 (L10, 10.8).`], [`60`, `60 does not come from the formula. τ(K_n) = nⁿ⁻² gives 5³ = 125.`], [`10`, `10 = 5 × 4 / 2 is the number of edges of K₅. Spanning trees: 5⁵⁻² = 125.`], [`25`, `25 = 5² uses exponent n − 3. The formula is nⁿ⁻² = 5³ = 125.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `A column of all zeros in a cycle matrix B(G) means the edge:`,
      o: [[`belongs to every cycle`, `An edge in every cycle would give a column of all ones. All zeros means the edge is in no cycle.`], [`belongs to no cycle`, `Correct. Row i of B(G) marks the edges of cycle i, so a column with no 1s is an edge lying in no cycle, a non-cycle edge (L10, 10.3 Observation 1).`], [`is a self-loop`, `A self-loop is a cycle by itself and gives a row with a single 1 (Observation 3), not a zero column.`], [`is parallel to another edge`, `Parallel edges form a cycle together (like Z1 = {e1, e2}), so their columns are not all zero.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `For the triangle K₃, Q = H − X = [2 −1 −1; −1 2 −1; −1 −1 2]. Any cofactor equals:`,
      o: [[`2`, `2 is a diagonal entry (the degree). A cofactor is a 2 × 2 determinant: 2·2 − (−1)(−1) = 3.`], [`4`, `4 = 2 × 2 forgets to subtract the off-diagonal product: det[2 −1; −1 2] = 4 − 1 = 3.`], [`3`, `Correct. Matrix-tree theorem: every cofactor of H − X equals τ(G). det[2 −1; −1 2] = 4 − 1 = 3 = τ(K₃) = 3³⁻² (L10, worked example).`], [`1`, `1 is only the subtracted product (−1)(−1). The cofactor is 4 − 1 = 3.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `In a connected graph, d(v_i, v_j) = 3. Which is true?`,
      o: [[`[X³]_ij = 0`, `d = 3 means there is a path of length 3, which is an edge sequence of length 3, so [X³]_ij ≥ 1, not 0.`], [`[X²]_ij ≠ 0`, `[X²]_ij ≠ 0 would give a sequence of length 2 between them, making the distance at most 2.`], [`[X]_ij ≠ 0`, `[X]_ij ≠ 0 means v_i and v_j are adjacent, distance 1, not 3.`], [`[X³]_ij ≠ 0 and it is the first non-zero power`, `Correct. Theorem 10.18: d(v_i, v_j) = k iff k is the smallest power with [Xᵏ]_ij ≠ 0; here X and X² are 0 at (i, j) and X³ is not (L10).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `Which product is always zero modulo 2 (same edge order, no self-loops)?`,
      o: [[`A Bᵀ`, `Correct. Theorem 10.9: a vertex and a cycle share 0 or 2 edges, so every row of A is orthogonal to every row of B: A Bᵀ ≡ 0 (mod 2) (L10).`], [`F Fᵀ`, `F Fᵀ = H − X (Theorem 10.19), with degrees on the diagonal, so it is not zero.`], [`A Aᵀ`, `A Aᵀ has the degrees on its diagonal and adjacency counts off it, so it is not zero.`], [`X²`, `X² counts edge sequences of length 2; its diagonal holds the degrees, so it is not zero.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `The reduced incidence matrix A_f is obtained by:`,
      o: [[`transposing A`, `Transposing swaps rows and columns but keeps all the information. A_f deletes one vertex row.`], [`deleting one row (the reference vertex)`, `Correct. Deleting any one row of A(G) gives the reduced incidence matrix A_f; the deleted vertex is the reference vertex, and the row can be rebuilt (L10, Rank results).`], [`deleting one column`, `A_f deletes a row (the reference vertex), not a column; deleting a column would lose an edge.`], [`taking A modulo 2`, `A is already a 0/1 matrix over GF(2). A_f is formed by deleting the reference vertex's row.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `In a path matrix P(u, v), a column of all ones means the edge:`,
      o: [[`is a loop`, `A loop is never part of a path, so its column would be all zeros. All ones means the edge is in every u–v path.`], [`lies in no u–v path`, `An edge in no u–v path gives a column of all zeros (Observation 1), not all ones.`], [`lies in every u–v path`, `Correct. Row i of P(u, v) is the ith u–v path, so a 1 in every row of a column means that edge lies in every u–v path (L10, 10.7 Observation 2).`], [`is isolated`, `Isolation applies to vertices, not edges. A column of all ones means the edge is in every u–v path.`]],
      a: 2 }
  ],
  11: [
    { q: `In proving 1 + 3 + 5 + … + (2n − 1) = n² by induction, the inductive step must show:`,
      o: [[`(k + 1)² = k²`, `(k + 1)² = k² is false for every k ≥ 0. The step adds the next odd term to k²: k² + (2k + 1) = (k + 1)².`], [`k² + (2k − 1) = k²`, `2k − 1 is the kth odd number, already in the sum. The (k + 1)th is 2(k + 1) − 1 = 2k + 1, and the target is (k + 1)².`], [`k² + (2k + 1) = (k + 1)²`, `Correct. The (k + 1)th odd number is 2(k + 1) − 1 = 2k + 1.`], [`1 = 1²`, `1 = 1² checks n = 1, which is the basis step. The inductive step goes from k to k + 1.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `In the inductive step for 1 + 2 + … + n = n(n + 1)/2, k(k + 1)/2 + (k + 1) simplifies to:`,
      o: [[`(k + 1)²/2`, `(k + 1)²/2 would need k(k + 1) + 2(k + 1) = (k + 1)², but factoring gives (k + 1)(k + 2), not (k + 1)(k + 1).`], [`k² + 1`, `k² + 1 is not an algebraic simplification of k(k + 1)/2 + (k + 1); e.g. k = 1 gives 1 + 2 = 3, but k² + 1 = 2.`], [`k(k + 2)/2`, `k(k + 2)/2 factors out k instead of (k + 1). Both terms share (k + 1): [k(k + 1) + 2(k + 1)]/2 = (k + 1)(k + 2)/2.`], [`(k + 1)(k + 2)/2`, `Correct. k(k + 1)/2 + (k + 1) = [k(k + 1) + 2(k + 1)]/2 = (k + 1)(k + 2)/2, which is the formula with n = k + 1 (L11, Proof of 1 + … + n).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `The base case of an induction proof checks:`,
      o: [[`the first value, usually P(1)`, `Correct. The basis step proves P(n₀) for the first value of the claim, usually n = 1; the inductive step then carries it forward (L11, Principle of induction).`], [`every value of n`, `Checking every n is impossible for infinitely many values; that is what the whole induction proves. The basis checks only the first value.`], [`that P(k) implies P(k + 1)`, `Showing P(k) → P(k + 1) is the inductive step. The basis checks the starting value, P(1).`], [`P(0) only, always`, `The basis is the first value of the claim, n₀; for "n ≥ 1" claims that is P(1), not P(0).`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `For 1 + 4 + 7 + … + (3n − 2), the (k + 1)th term is:`,
      o: [[`3k + 2`, `3k + 2 = 3(k + 1) − 1, the factor in the formula (k + 1)(3k + 2)/2, not the term. The term is 3(k + 1) − 2 = 3k + 1.`], [`3k + 1`, `Correct. Substitute n = k + 1 into the general term 3n − 2: 3(k + 1) − 2 = 3k + 1 (L11, Proof of 1 + 4 + … + (3n − 2)).`], [`3k − 2`, `3k − 2 is the kth term, the last one already in the hypothesis. The next term is 3(k + 1) − 2 = 3k + 1.`], [`3k`, `3k = 3(k + 1) − 3 subtracts 3 instead of 2. The (k + 1)th term is 3k + 1.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `A = {1, 2}, B = {a, b, c}. How many binary relations from A to B exist?`,
      o: [[`8`, `8 = 2³ counts the subsets of B, not of A × B. A × B has 2 × 3 = 6 pairs, giving 2⁶ = 64 relations.`], [`5`, `5 = 2 + 3 adds the sizes. Any subset of the 6-pair set A × B is a relation: 2⁶ = 64.`], [`64`, `Correct. Every subset of A × B is a relation: 2⁶.`], [`6`, `6 = |A × B| counts the ordered pairs. A relation is any subset of those 6 pairs, so there are 2⁶ = 64.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `On A = {1, 2, 3}, the identity relation I_A is:`,
      o: [[`A × A`, `A × A contains all 9 pairs, including (1, 2) etc.; that is the universal relation. I_A keeps only the pairs (a, a).`], [`{1, 2, 3}`, `{1, 2, 3} is A itself, a set of numbers. A relation must be a set of ordered pairs: {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3)}.`], [`{(1,2), (2,3)}`, `{(1,2), (2,3)} pairs each element with a different one and misses (a, a). The identity relation is {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3)}.`], [`{(1,1), (2,2), (3,3)}`, `Correct. I_A = {(a, a) | a ∈ A} pairs each element with itself, so on {1, 2, 3} it is {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3)} (L11, Binary relations).`]],
      a: 3 },
    { q: `R = {(1,1), (1,2), (2,1)} on A = {1, 2} is:`,
      o: [[`symmetric`, `Correct. Symmetric means (a, b) ∈ R → (b, a) ∈ R: (1,2) has its partner (2,1), and (1,1) is its own reverse (L11, Properties of relations).`], [`the identity relation`, `The identity relation on {1, 2} is {(1,1), (2,2)}; R contains (1,2) and (2,1) and lacks (2,2).`], [`antisymmetric`, `Antisymmetric requires (a, b) and (b, a) both in R only when a = b, but (1,2) and (2,1) are both in R with 1 ≠ 2.`], [`reflexive`, `Reflexive needs (a, a) for every a ∈ A; (2,2) is missing, so R is not reflexive.`]],
      a: 0 },
    { q: `If R is a relation from A to B, then R is a subset of:`,
      o: [[`B × A`, `B × A holds pairs (b, a). A relation from A to B uses pairs (a, b), so R ⊆ A × B.`], [`A × B`, `Correct. By definition a binary relation from A to B is a subset of the Cartesian product A × B, a set of ordered pairs (a, b) (L11, Binary relations).`], [`A ∩ B`, `A ∩ B is a set of single elements common to both, not of ordered pairs. A relation is a subset of A × B.`], [`A ∪ B`, `A ∪ B contains single elements, but a relation contains ordered pairs, so it is a subset of A × B.`]],
      a: 1 },
    { q: `Congruence modulo n (a ≡ b mod n) is an example of:`,
      o: [[`an antisymmetric relation`, `3 ≡ 8 and 8 ≡ 3 (mod 5) both hold with 3 ≠ 8, so congruence is not antisymmetric; it is symmetric.`], [`the empty relation`, `Congruence mod n is far from empty: every a ≡ a (mod n), and 3 ≡ 8 (mod 5) is a lecture example.`], [`an equivalence relation`, `Correct. Reflexive, symmetric and transitive.`], [`a function only`, `Congruence relates a to many b (3 ≡ 8 ≡ 13 mod 5), so it is not a function. It is an equivalence relation.`]],
      a: 2 },
    { q: `To prove 2ⁿ &gt; n for n ≥ 1, the inductive step uses:`,
      o: [[`2ᵏ⁺¹ = 2ᵏ + 1`, `2ᵏ⁺¹ = 2 · 2ᵏ, not 2ᵏ + 1 (e.g. 2² = 4 but 2¹ + 1 = 3). The step doubles: 2ᵏ⁺¹ = 2·2ᵏ &gt; 2k.`], [`2ᵏ &lt; k`, `2ᵏ &lt; k contradicts the hypothesis 2ᵏ &gt; k; the step must use the hypothesis, not its opposite.`], [`2¹ &gt; 1`, `2¹ &gt; 1 is the basis step (n = 1). The inductive step goes from k to k + 1: 2ᵏ⁺¹ = 2·2ᵏ &gt; 2k ≥ k + 1.`], [`2ᵏ⁺¹ = 2·2ᵏ &gt; 2k ≥ k + 1`, `Correct. Uses the hypothesis 2ᵏ &gt; k and k ≥ 1.`]],
      a: 3 }
  ]
};
